[Mailchimp](https://mailchimp.com) is an email marketing platform. You keep your subscribers in an audience, and signup forms on your website add new contacts to it, so you can send them newsletters and campaigns.

# How does the integration work?

The **Magic Portfolio** template for [Appwrite Sites](/docs/products/sites), a Next.js portfolio built with Once UI, includes a newsletter signup form. The form in `src/components/Mailchimp.tsx` posts the visitor's email address to the URL in `mailchimp.action`, which is set in `src/app/resources/config.js`. The template ships with a placeholder there, `https://url/subscribe/post?parameters`, so signups only reach Mailchimp after you replace it with your audience's form action URL.

The form appears on the home page and the blog page whenever `newsletter.display` is `true` in `src/app/resources/content.js`.

# How to implement

To collect newsletter signups with Mailchimp on Appwrite Sites, there are several steps you must complete:

## Step 1: Deploy Magic Portfolio with a repository

For this step, you must [create an account on Appwrite Cloud](/sign-up) or [self-host Appwrite](/docs/advanced/self-hosting) if you haven't already. In your Appwrite project, go to **Sites**, click **Create site**, search the templates for **Magic Portfolio**, and choose **Connect your repository** so you can edit the code. The [Magic Portfolio integration guide](/integrations/sites-magic-portfolio) walks through each screen.

## Step 2: Find your Mailchimp form action URL

Mailchimp accepts signups from your own HTML form at a URL that names your account and audience:

```text
https://<server>.list-manage.com/subscribe/post?u=<user-id>&id=<audience-id>
```

Mailchimp's guide to [hosting your own signup forms](https://mailchimp.com/help/host-your-own-signup-forms/) shows where to find the server, the `u` value, and the `id` value in the source of your audience's hosted signup form.

## Step 3: Connect the form to your audience

Clone the repository and set the action URL in `src/app/resources/config.js`:

```js
const mailchimp = {
  action: "https://<server>.list-manage.com/subscribe/post?u=<user-id>&id=<audience-id>",
  // effects: ...
};
```

`src/components/Mailchimp.tsx` also contains two hidden fields copied from the original author's audience: a bot trap input named `b_c1a5a210340eb6c7bff33b2ba_0462d244aa` and an interest group checkbox named `group[3492][1]`. Replace them with the matching fields from your own audience's signup form code, or remove them.

To change the heading and description above the form, edit `newsletter` in `src/app/resources/content.js`. Set `display` to `false` to hide the form. Commit and push to the production branch, and Appwrite builds and activates a new deployment.

## Step 4: Test the integration

Open your deployed site, enter an email address in the newsletter form, and click **Subscribe**. Then open your audience in Mailchimp and check that the contact was added, or is waiting for confirmation if your audience uses double opt-in.

